    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">Non-alcoholic beverages can be further classified into several sub-groups based on their main ingredient or method of preparation.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Classes of Non-Alcoholic Beverages</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Examples</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stimulating beverages</td><td>Tea, coffee, cocoa</td><td>Contain mild stimulants (caffeine, theobromine) that refresh and increase alertness</td></tr><tr><td>Nourishing (milk) beverages</td><td>Milk, milk shakes, Milo/Bournvita drinks, egg flip</td><td>Provide protein, calcium, vitamins and energy; suitable for growing children and convalescents</td></tr><tr><td>Refreshing beverages</td><td>Fruit juices, squashes, fruit drinks, soft drinks</td><td>Quench thirst and supply vitamins (especially vitamin C) and natural or added sugars</td></tr><tr><td>Soft drinks/carbonated beverages</td><td>Cola, lemonade, tonic water</td><td>Carbonated, often sweetened, provide quick energy but little other nutritive value</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Justification with Examples</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li><strong>Tea and coffee</strong> are classed as stimulating beverages because their caffeine content increases alertness and reduces fatigue, though they have little food value on their own.</li><li><strong>Milk-based drinks</strong> are classed as nourishing beverages because milk supplies high-quality protein, calcium and vitamins A and B group.</li><li><strong>Fresh fruit juices</strong> are classed as refreshing beverages because they are consumed mainly to quench thirst while providing vitamin C.</li><li><strong>Carbonated soft drinks</strong> are classed separately because carbon dioxide gas is dissolved in them for fizz, and they are usually high in sugar but low in other nutrients.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Preparation Considerations</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Beverages should be prepared hygienically, using clean water and equipment.</li><li>Hot beverages (tea, coffee, cocoa) should be made with freshly boiled water for best flavour.</li><li>Fruit juices should be prepared close to serving time to preserve vitamin C, which is easily destroyed by exposure to air and light.</li><li>Milk-based beverages should not be boiled for too long, as this can destroy heat-sensitive vitamins and cause the milk to curdle.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to justify WHY a drink belongs to a particular class, not just name the class — WAEC often asks for reasons, not just classification.</div>",
