    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">Proper <span class=\"learn-keyword\">table setting</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">table etiquette</span> ensure that meals are served and eaten in a manner that is both functional and courteous, reflecting good hospitality practice.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Key Roles in Entertaining</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Host/Hostess</td><td>The person(s) organising and welcoming guests to the occasion</td></tr><tr><td>Guest</td><td>The person(s) invited to attend the occasion</td></tr><tr><td>Waiter/Waitress</td><td>The person(s) responsible for serving food and drinks during the occasion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Qualities of a Good Host/Hostess</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Welcoming, friendly and attentive to guests' needs.</li><li>Well organised, with the event properly planned in advance.</li><li>Able to introduce guests to one another where appropriate.</li><li>Patient and calm, even if something goes wrong.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Qualities of a Good Guest</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Punctual, arriving at the agreed time.</li><li>Courteous to the host and other guests.</li><li>Observant of table manners and etiquette.</li><li>Appreciative, thanking the host appropriately.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Qualities of a Good Waiter/Waitress</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Clean, neatly dressed and well presented.</li><li>Attentive but unobtrusive in service.</li><li>Knowledgeable about the menu being served.</li><li>Polite, patient and efficient.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Table Setting: Formal vs Informal</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Formal table setting</th><th>Informal table setting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full cutlery and glassware set in a specific order, course by course</td><td>Simpler arrangement with only necessary cutlery for the meal</td></tr><tr><td>Tablecloth, napkins (often cloth), place cards sometimes used</td><td>Table mats or simple covering; paper napkins often acceptable</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple courses with corresponding cutlery (e.g. soup spoon, fish fork)</td><td>Usually fewer courses, with cutlery limited to what is needed</td></tr><tr><td>Used for formal dinners, receptions, official occasions</td><td>Used for everyday family meals or casual gatherings</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Table Manners and Etiquette</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Wait until everyone is served (or the host signals) before starting to eat.</li><li>Use cutlery from the outside in, course by course, in formal settings.</li><li>Chew with the mouth closed and avoid talking with food in the mouth.</li><li>Place the napkin on the lap and use it to dab, not wipe vigorously.</li><li>Avoid reaching across others; ask politely for items to be passed.</li><li>Signal the end of a course by placing cutlery together on the plate.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to list differences between formal and informal table settings in a comparison table — this is a common WAEC question format.</div>",
