    "topic": "Self-Employment: Work Ethics and Food Packaging",
    "topicCode": "SS3-FN-12",
    "module": "Catering and Enterprise",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Work ethics</span> describes the professional standards and attitudes expected in catering work, while <span class=\"learn-keyword\">food packaging</span> protects and presents food for sale, storage or transport.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Work Ethics</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Work ethics refers to the moral principles and standards of conduct that guide behaviour in the workplace, including honesty, punctuality, diligence, hygiene and professionalism.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Elements of Good Work Ethics in Catering</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Punctuality and reliability in meeting orders and deadlines.</li><li>Strict personal and food hygiene at all times.</li><li>Honesty in pricing, ingredient quality and business dealings.</li><li>Respect for customers, colleagues and suppliers.</li><li>Consistency in food quality and presentation.</li><li>Willingness to learn and adapt to feedback.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Meaning of Food Packaging</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Food packaging is the material or container used to wrap, contain or protect food products for storage, sale, transport or presentation.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Reasons for Packaging Food</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Protects food from contamination, dirt and pests.</li><li>Preserves freshness and extends shelf life.</li><li>Prevents physical damage during handling and transport.</li><li>Provides important information (ingredients, date, weight) to the consumer.</li><li>Enhances presentation and brand appeal, supporting marketing.</li><li>Makes food easier to portion, carry and store.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Types and Qualities of Packaging Materials</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Qualities / Suitable uses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Paper and cardboard</td><td>Lightweight, biodegradable, suitable for dry foods and takeaway boxes</td></tr><tr><td>Plastic (food-grade)</td><td>Durable, moisture-resistant, suitable for liquids and perishables, but less eco-friendly</td></tr><tr><td>Glass</td><td>Non-reactive, reusable, good for preserves and liquids, but fragile and heavy</td></tr><tr><td>Aluminium foil/cans</td><td>Good barrier against light and air, suitable for preserved and heat-sealed foods</td></tr><tr><td>Leaves (traditional)</td><td>Biodegradable, used traditionally for wrapping foods such as moin-moin or eba</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Qualities of Good Food Packaging</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Food-safe (non-toxic, does not react with the food).</li><li>Strong enough to protect the contents during handling.</li><li>Appropriately sized for the quantity of food.</li><li>Clearly labelled with relevant information.</li><li>Attractive, to support marketing and sales.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to match a type of food (e.g. hot soup, dry snacks, liquid drinks) to the most suitable packaging material and explain why.</div>",
