    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Experimental cookery</span> involves creating, testing and refining recipes, often as part of research, product development or practical coursework in Food and Nutrition.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of a Recipe</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">A recipe is a set of written instructions specifying the ingredients and method required to prepare a particular dish, usually including quantities, preparation steps and cooking times.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Sources of Recipe Information</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Cookery books and magazines.</li><li>Family members and community elders (oral tradition).</li><li>The internet and cookery websites or apps.</li><li>Television cookery programmes.</li><li>Professional chefs and culinary training institutions.</li><li>Food product packaging (manufacturer's suggested recipes).</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Methods of Collecting Recipe Information</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Interviewing experienced cooks or family members.</li><li>Observing demonstrations by skilled cooks or chefs.</li><li>Reading and studying written recipes from books or the internet.</li><li>Trial and experimentation, recording results and adjustments.</li><li>Attending cookery classes or workshops.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Importance of Collecting Recipe Information</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Ensures accuracy and consistency when preparing a dish.</li><li>Preserves traditional recipes for future generations.</li><li>Supports the development of new or improved dishes.</li><li>Provides a reliable reference for quantities and timing, reducing wastage.</li><li>Builds a personal or institutional recipe collection for future use.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Advantages of Having Recipes</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Saves time by avoiding repeated trial and error.</li><li>Ensures consistent quality and taste each time a dish is made.</li><li>Makes it easier to scale a dish up or down for different numbers of servings.</li><li>Assists in accurate costing and budgeting for catering purposes.</li><li>Allows dishes to be shared and replicated by others accurately.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Creating a Recipe</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Creating a recipe involves: deciding on the dish and its purpose, listing all ingredients with accurate quantities, writing clear, ordered preparation and cooking steps, testing the recipe in practice, and adjusting quantities, timing or method based on the results before finalising it.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to outline the steps for developing and testing a new recipe in logical order — this process is often examined directly.</div>",
