    "topic": "Interpreting Cookery Practical Questions",
    "topicCode": "SS3-FN-14",
    "module": "Practical Skills and Assessment",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">A key WASSCE Food and Nutrition practical skill is the ability to correctly interpret a given cookery question and plan an appropriate response before any cooking begins.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Why Interpretation Matters</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Cookery practical questions often specify a dish category, number of servings, special requirement (e.g. for a diabetic guest) or time limit. Misreading these details can lead to an entirely unsuitable dish being prepared, even if the cooking itself is skilfully done.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Steps in Interpreting a Cookery Question</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Read the question carefully, more than once, to identify exactly what is required.</li><li>Underline or note key details: dish type, number of servings, special dietary requirement, equipment allowed, and time available.</li><li>Identify any implied requirements (e.g. a question for 'an invalid' implies a bland, easily digestible dish).</li><li>Select a suitable dish that meets all stated requirements.</li><li>List the ingredients and quantities needed, scaled correctly to the number of servings specified.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Writing a Time and Work Plan</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Order of work</td><td>Lists tasks in the sequence they should be carried out, most time-consuming or longest-cooking items started first</td></tr><tr><td>Time allocation</td><td>Assigns an estimated time to each task, fitting within the total time available</td></tr><tr><td>Dovetailing</td><td>Planning tasks so that while one dish cooks unattended, another can be actively prepared, to use time efficiently</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Common Errors in Interpretation</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Ignoring a stated dietary restriction (e.g. preparing a sugary dessert for a diabetic guest).</li><li>Preparing the wrong number of servings.</li><li>Failing to plan time efficiently, leading to rushed or incomplete dishes.</li><li>Choosing a dish that cannot realistically be completed within the given time or with the equipment specified.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always write out your interpretation and work plan clearly before cooking begins — examiners assess your planning and understanding, not only the finished dish.</div>",
