    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">Building on the skill of interpreting cookery questions, this topic focuses on preparing complete, detailed cooking schedules in response to a given assignment.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. From Interpretation to Schedule</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Once a cookery question has been correctly interpreted and a suitable dish selected, the next step is to produce a detailed, realistic schedule showing exactly how the task will be completed within the time and resources available.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Elements of a Cooking Schedule</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Menu/dish list</td><td>The specific dish(es) to be prepared, as determined from interpreting the question</td></tr><tr><td>Ingredients and quantities</td><td>A complete list, scaled correctly for the required number of servings</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment list</td><td>All tools, utensils and appliances needed</td></tr><tr><td>Order of work with timing</td><td>A step-by-step sequence with the time each step should start and how long it should take</td></tr><tr><td>Serving plan</td><td>How and when the finished dish will be plated, garnished and presented</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Principles of a Good Cooking Schedule</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Start with tasks that take the longest (e.g. items requiring long simmering or baking).</li><li>Group similar tasks together where efficient (e.g. all chopping done at once).</li><li>Build in time for washing up and clearing as you go, where possible.</li><li>Allow a small buffer of time before the final serving deadline.</li><li>Ensure the schedule is realistic and achievable within the stated time limit.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Common Mistakes in Cooking Schedules</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Underestimating the time needed for certain cooking processes.</li><li>Scheduling tasks that require the same equipment at the same time.</li><li>Failing to account for resting, cooling or setting time for some dishes.</li><li>Leaving presentation and garnishing until the very last, rushed moment.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A well-written cooking schedule should allow an examiner to see, at a glance, exactly what you planned to do and when — clarity and logical sequencing earn marks independently of the cooking itself.</div>",
